  <!-- H1 -->
  <h1 class="page-h1">How to Add a Spouse to a Deed in Florida (and Why the Tenancy Form Matters)</h1>

  <p class="last-updated">Updated August 7, 2026 using the primary authorities cited below.</p>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <p>
    Adding your spouse to your home's deed is one of the most common real estate moves Florida homeowners
    make — and one of the easiest to get subtly wrong. The mechanics are straightforward: you prepare a
    Florida quitclaim deed, sign it in front of two witnesses and a notary, and record it with the county
    clerk. But the line in the deed that describes <em>how</em> you and your spouse will hold title is
    where important ownership, survivorship and creditor-rights consequences can arise.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Add Spouse -->
  <h2>Why Homeowners Add a Spouse to the Deed</h2>
  <p>There are several practical reasons you might want both names on title:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Survivorship rights.</strong> If only one spouse owns the home and that spouse dies, the
      property passes through their estate — potentially through probate. Adding the other spouse with
      the right survivorship language keeps the home out of the probate process entirely.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Estate planning.</strong> Joint ownership with survivorship is one of the simplest
      estate-planning tools available. It eliminates the need for a separate transfer after death and
      reduces the paperwork a surviving spouse faces during an already difficult time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refinancing requirements.</strong> Some lenders require both spouses to appear on title
      before they will approve a refinance or new loan.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Equitable interest.</strong> If both spouses contribute financially to a home, having only
      one name on title can create complications in matters of tax filing, insurance, or any future sale.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- How It Works -->
  <h2>How a Quitclaim Deed Works for This Transfer</h2>
  <p>
    A quitclaim deed conveys whatever interest the grantor currently has, without warranties about title
    history. For adding a spouse, the structure is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grantor:</strong> the current sole owner (the spouse already on title)</li>
    <li><strong>Grantee:</strong> the current owner AND the new spouse together</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The grantor is essentially conveying the property to themselves jointly with their spouse. Florida
    law permits this — a person can deed property to themselves and another party in a single instrument.
  </p>
  <p>
    The critical piece is the vesting language. Simply naming two people as grantees without any tenancy
    language creates a tenancy in common by default in Florida, which may not match the couple's intended
    survivorship or ownership arrangement.
  </p>

  <!-- Primary CTA -->
